.UPDATE...
Issued at 950 AM PDT Tue Oct 13 2026

Water vapor imagery shows an upper level low digging into the
Great Basin this morning, with high pressure over the Pacific. As
a result, Northern California remains situated between the two,
with dry northerly flow aloft. Given the predominately clear
skies, cooler air, and the light winds winds, most locations saw
morning temperatures fall into the 40s or lower 50s, with the
coldest spots dipping into the 30s. Fall is definitely in the air. That
said, as this low shifts east, high pressure will build into the
area, with warmer conditions prevailing. Today will be the start
of that warming trend, despite the colder start to the day. Expect
temperatures to increase a few degrees each day. Overnight
temperatures will also see a minor warming trend as well this
week, but still expect the cool fall like temperatures through at
least Wednesday night.

Periods of light to moderate offshore winds will also develop the
next several nights and continue through the early morning hours
across the North and East Bay Mountains and Hills. While these
gusty winds will combine with poor to moderate humidity
recoveries, the duration and speeds should remain below red flag
thresholds. That said, always remember one less spark. Current
forecast looks on track, so no updates are planned at this time. Palmer

