.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1119 PM PDT Mon Oct 12 2026
(Tonight through Wednesday)

Expect one more cooler taste of fall on Tuesday before a warming
trend starts off Wednesday. Upper level troughing will gradually
shift eastward Tuesday into Wednesday with upper level ridging
building in Wednesday. The marine layer remains mixed out through
the short term with clear skies expected across the majority of the
area with only patchy clouds expected along the coast. Clear skies
will lead to increased radiational cooling and see chillier mornings
in the 40s to low 50s returning again Tuesday and Wednesday
mornings. Portions of the higher elevations will dip into the 30s
but this will be more widespread on Tuesday than on Wednesday as a
warming trend begins. Daytime highs on Tuesday will be in the 60s to
mid 70s with further warming into the 70s to low 80s across the
interior expected on Wednesday as the ridge rebuilds. Coastal
temperatures stay fairly consistent in the upper 50s to 60s.

Worth noting is the potential for multiple rounds of offshore winds
in the overnight to late morning hours starting tonight and
continuing through the week. Offshore winds look to be the strongest
across the Interior North Bay Mountains and along the Diablo Range
in the East Bay and South Bay. The SFO-WMC gradient suggests rounds
of overnight offshore winds Monday night and Tuesday night will be
light to moderate with the gradient peaking around -3 to -4.5 mb.
The gradient is expected to strengthen late week as additional
rounds of offshore winds occur (see Long Term discussion).
Elevated fire weather conditions are expected across the higher
elevations given the moderate pulses of offshore winds, dry
conditions, and poor overnight recoveries.

