.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1119 PM PDT Mon Oct 12 2026
(Wednesday night through next Monday)

Temperatures continue to warm Thursday and Friday as upper level
ridging builds. High temperatures will peak in the upper 80s to low
90s with pockets of mid to upper 90s across the Santa Cruz Mountains
and southern Santa Clara Valley. Patchy Moderate HeatRisk is
expected on Thursday with more widespread Moderate HeatRisk across
urban areas on Friday. Although not the hottest temperatures we`ve
seen this summer, Moderate HeatRisk makes its return due to
overnight lows creeping back into the 50s to low 60s. Not a fan of
the heat? Good news, temperatures start to cool again Saturday and
Sunday as upper level ridging is disrupted by a cut-off low to our
south. This cut-off low will rejoin the jet stream, deepening upper
level troughing over the West Coast, and bring cooler weather back
to the region. High temperatures drop into the 80s across the
interior on Saturday before more fall like weather in the 60s to 70s
returns Sunday and Monday. Model guidance indicates there is some
potential for light rain (few tenths of an inch) and strong onshore
winds early next week as the trough moves through. This will bring a
return of hazardous marine conditions and strong winds to mountain
gaps and passes and the higher terrain.

Elevated fire weather concerns continue across the higher elevations
through late this week as additional rounds of offshore flow are
expected overnight through the late morning. These pulses of
offshore winds look to strengthen overnight Wednesday into
Thursday. The GFS shows an initial forecast for the SFO-WMC
gradient to reach -9.6 mb late Thursday morning. Offshore flow is
likely again Thursday night into Friday morning but it looks to be
weaker than the previous night. If you are engaging in any
activities involving fire, prevent any flames/sparks from
escaping as the fuels are dry and conditions will be favorable for
fire to spread.

