.FIRE WEATHER...
Issued at 118 PM PDT Mon Oct 12 2026

There will be a few bursts of offshore winds this week, generally
during the overnight hours. The first burst looks to occur tonight,
particularly highlight the interior North Bay Mountains, where we
could see gusts range from 20-35 mph, perhaps even up to 40 mph for
the exposed ridges and the higher elevations. After that, the next
area of concern we are monitoring is Wednesday night into Thursday
morning, where the pressure gradient should be a bit more tight due
to upper level wind patterns. Majority of the area should see
offshore flow, in the north to northeast component, with gusts
closer to 25-35 mph, locally higher perhaps up to 45 mph for
exposed ridges, gaps, and passes. Warm and dry weather will be on
tap as well, which will lead to an elevated threat for the higher
terrain areas. Another round of offshore winds is expected
Thursday night into Friday, but appears to be weaker than the
previous two.

